# RabbitMQ Metrics Integration with Azure Managed Prometheus

## Objective

Expose RabbitMQ Prometheus metrics and make them available in:

- Azure Managed Prometheus
- Azure Managed Grafana

---

## Current Situation

We already verified that RabbitMQ is exposing Prometheus metrics.

Test:

```bash
curl http://20.37.131.226:15692/metrics | head
```

Example output:

```text
# HELP erlang_mnesia_held_locks Number of held locks.
# TYPE erlang_mnesia_held_locks gauge
erlang_mnesia_held_locks 0
```

This confirms:

```text
RabbitMQ Prometheus Plugin = Enabled
Metrics Endpoint = Working
```

---

## Important Concept

Exposing metrics does NOT mean Prometheus is collecting them.

Observability flow:

```text
RabbitMQ
    |
    | /metrics
    v
Azure Managed Prometheus
    |
    v
Azure Monitor Workspace
    |
    v
Azure Managed Grafana
```

Currently we have:

```text
RabbitMQ
    |
    v
Metrics Endpoint
```

The next step is:

```text
Azure Managed Prometheus
      |
      v
Scrape RabbitMQ
```

---

# Step 1 - Verify Monitoring CRDs

Check whether PodMonitor or ServiceMonitor CRDs exist.

```bash
kubectl get crd | grep monitoring.coreos.com
```

Expected:

```text
podmonitors.monitoring.coreos.com
servicemonitors.monitoring.coreos.com
```

---

# Step 2 - Verify RabbitMQ Labels

```bash
kubectl get pods -n $NS --show-labels | grep rabbitmq
```

Expected label:

```text
app=rabbitmq
```

---

# Step 3 - Verify RabbitMQ Metrics Service

Verify the service exposing metrics.

```bash
kubectl get svc rabbitmq-prometheus -n $NS
```

Detailed view:

```bash
kubectl describe svc rabbitmq-prometheus -n $NS
```

Verify:

```text
Port: 15692
TargetPort: 15692
```

---

# Step 4 - Create PodMonitor

Create:

```yaml
cat <<EOF > rabbitmq-podmonitor.yaml
apiVersion: azmonitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: PodMonitor
metadata:
  name: rabbitmq
  namespace: $NS
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: rabbitmq
  podMetricsEndpoints:
  - port: prometheus
    path: /metrics
    interval: 30s
EOF
```

Apply:

```bash
kubectl apply -f rabbitmq-podmonitor.yaml
```

---

# Step 5 - Validate PodMonitor

```bash
kubectl get podmonitor -n $NS
```

Expected:

```text
rabbitmq
```

Inspect:

```bash
kubectl describe podmonitor rabbitmq -n $NS
```

---

# Step 6 - Wait for Discovery

Azure Managed Prometheus typically requires:

```text
2–5 minutes
```

to discover the new scrape target.

---

# Step 7 - Open Grafana Explore

Open:

```text
Azure Managed Grafana
  → Explore
```

Test:

```promql
up
```

Look for:

```text
job="rabbitmq"
```

---

